This PR allows the usage of an input file and a parameter of size in GB that allows a manual/external control of the script runs.

It keeps track of uploaded files given the input CSV and use its data to determine if the usage quota in GB will be met, and if so the execution is interrupted until the next run.

The use case that inspired this implementation is:
- A migration of a large amount of data of the Zoom Cloud into Google Drive (> 2 TB);
- The user wanted to fine control the upload quota of Google Drive, so its services don't get interrupted;
- The set of recording is a finite known number 1<x<10000.
- The executable should be as self-contained as possible (don't expect the usage of queues, brokers, external DBs, etc);
- The execution should be idempotent, with fail recovery support for e.g. network failures;




---

## TODO

- [x] add a feature that checks if the recording was already uploaded without rely on the log file, i.e. build the row hash on using the CSV data then build it using GDrive data and compare them to determine if the upload is already present;

- [x] add a feature to delete recordings of Zoom Cloud given a date;

---

The migration is meant to be made in 3 phases:

1. Move data from Zoom Cloud to Google Drive
2. Audit, check, verify the migrated files
3. Delete recordings from Zoom Cloud

